---
import '../../../styles/global.css';
import OriginalRoundLab from '../../../components/artifacts/speculative-decoding/OriginalRoundLab.tsx';
import SamplingLab from '../../../components/artifacts/speculative-decoding/SamplingLab.tsx';
import MedusaTreeLab from '../../../components/artifacts/speculative-decoding/MedusaTreeLab.tsx';
import EagleFeatureLab from '../../../components/artifacts/speculative-decoding/EagleFeatureLab.tsx';
import LayerSkipLab from '../../../components/artifacts/speculative-decoding/LayerSkipLab.tsx';
import PerformanceLab from '../../../components/artifacts/speculative-decoding/PerformanceLab.tsx';
import Sources from '../../../components/Sources.astro';
import ThemeScript from '../../../components/ThemeScript.astro';
import ThemeToggle from '../../../components/ThemeToggle.astro';

const sources = [
  {
    title: 'Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding',
    url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.17192',
    author: 'Leviathan, Kalman, and Matias',
    note: 'First submitted 30 November 2022; ICML 2023. Original chain algorithm and exact sampling.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Accelerating Large Language Model Decoding with Speculative Sampling',
    url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.01318',
    author: 'Chen and colleagues',
    note: 'First submitted 2 February 2023. Parallel scoring and modified rejection sampling.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Medusa: Simple LLM Inference Acceleration Framework with Multiple Decoding Heads',
    url: 'https://arxiv.org/html/2401.10774v3',
    note: 'Heads, tree attention, training strategies, and acceptance choices.',
  },
  {
    title: 'EAGLE: Speculative Sampling Requires Rethinking Feature Uncertainty',
    url: 'https://arxiv.org/html/2401.15077v3',
    note: 'Original feature-based drafting with shifted tokens and tree verification.',
  },
  {
    title: 'LayerSkip: Enabling Early Exit Inference and Self-Speculative Decoding',
    url: 'https://arxiv.org/html/2404.16710v4',
    note: 'Training, shared early-layer work, verification, and cache reuse.',
  },
];

const title = 'Speculative Decoding';
const description =
  'Learn original speculative decoding, Medusa, EAGLE, and LayerSkip with step-by-step interactive labs.';

const contents = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'Why guess ahead?' },
  { id: 'original', label: 'Original method: a small model drafts, the target checks' },
  { id: 'verify', label: 'How can verification be parallel?' },
  { id: 'sampling', label: 'Sampling needs a different acceptance rule' },
  { id: 'medusa', label: 'Medusa: predict future positions with extra heads' },
  { id: 'eagle', label: 'EAGLE: predict features with the selected token in view' },
  { id: 'layerskip', label: 'LayerSkip: use early layers as the drafter' },
  { id: 'performance', label: 'Choose a method by cost, not by its name' },
  { id: 'recap', label: 'Teach back the whole mechanism' },
  { id: 'sources', label: 'Sources and reading paths' },
];

const label = 'font-label text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]';
const boxTitle = 'font-display text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]';
const formula = 'border border-neutral-500 bg-white px-4 py-3 text-center font-label text-sm';
const questionCls = 'cursor-pointer font-display text-sm font-bold';
const cite = 'underline underline-offset-4 hover:no-underline';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site)} />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <ThemeScript />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-white font-body text-neutral-900 antialiased">
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-5 pb-12 pt-10 sm:pt-16">
      <nav class="flex items-center justify-between font-label text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]">
        <a href="/" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:no-underline">← Maula Irfani</a>
        <ThemeToggle />
      </nav>

      <header class="mt-6 border-t-[6px] border-black pt-3">
        <p class={label}>AI engineering · A guided exploration</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-bold leading-[1.1] tracking-tight">{title}</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          Guess ahead. Check together. Commit only what the target allows.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 font-body leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          Follow the original method through one complete round. Then explore how Medusa, EAGLE, and LayerSkip change
          the drafter without making verification disappear.
        </p>
        <dl class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-[115px_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-sm">
          <dt class="font-display font-bold">Before reading</dt>
          <dd>Know that a language model processes tokens and produces next-token scores. Basic causal attention is helpful.</dd>
          <dt class="font-display font-bold">You will learn</dt>
          <dd>Why verification can be parallel, how rejection works, where drafts come from, and when speculation is worth its cost.</dd>
          <dt class="font-display font-bold">Scope</dt>
          <dd>Original chain speculation, exact sampling, and three representative variants. Not an implementation runbook or exhaustive survey.</dd>
          <dt class="font-display font-bold">How to read</dt>
          <dd>Read the original flow first. Use the labs to inspect mechanisms; open the optional details when you want more depth.</dd>
        </dl>
      </header>

      <nav class="mt-10 border-t border-black pt-4" aria-labelledby="contents-title">
        <h2 id="contents-title" class={label}>Contents</h2>
        <ol class="mt-3 grid gap-x-8 text-sm sm:grid-cols-2">
          {
            contents.map((c, i) => (
              <li class="border-b border-neutral-300 py-1.5">
                <a href={`#${c.id}`} class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:no-underline">
                  <span class="font-label text-neutral-500">{i + 1}.</span> {c.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- Chapter 1 -->
      <section id="problem" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 1</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">Why guess ahead?</h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            You ask a language model to continue a sentence. It chooses one token, feeds that token back, and chooses
            another. That serial dependency is real: a different first token can change everything that follows.
          </p>
          <p>
            Speculative decoding does not remove the dependency. It uses a cheaper mechanism to propose a continuation,
            then asks the target model to check several positions together. If the guesses are good, one expensive
            evaluation can advance the answer by several tokens.
          </p>
          <p>
            The original method can use an existing smaller model as the drafter, without retraining or changing the
            target model. The early papers by Leviathan, Kalman, and Matias and by Chen and colleagues explain how
            verification and corrected sampling preserve the target distribution.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[1]</a> <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[2]</a>
          </p>

          <aside class="border border-l-[7px] border-black bg-white px-4 py-3">
            <p class={boxTitle}>The key mental model</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
              Unknown future tokens must first be chosen. Candidate tokens are already known, so their conditional
              predictions can be evaluated together. A guess is an input to a check, not an authorized output.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <section class="mt-8 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Four terms, four responsibilities</h3>
          <dl class="space-y-3">
            <div>
              <dt class="font-display font-bold text-neutral-900">Target model</dt>
              <dd class="mt-0.5">The model whose decoding behavior we want to preserve.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="font-display font-bold text-neutral-900">Drafter</dt>
              <dd class="mt-0.5">A cheaper proposal mechanism. It can be a separate model, trained heads, or an early-exit path.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="font-display font-bold text-neutral-900">Drafting</dt>
              <dd class="mt-0.5">The proposal stage only. A small autoregressive drafter may still generate its candidates sequentially.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="font-display font-bold text-neutral-900">Verification</dt>
              <dd class="mt-0.5">Target evaluation of the proposed continuation, followed by an acceptance rule.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            We will first use greedy decoding, where the highest-scoring target token is chosen. Sampling comes later.
            All sentence examples treat each displayed word as one token for clarity; real tokenization may split words
            differently.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 2 -->
      <section id="original" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 2</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">
          Original method: a small model drafts, the target checks
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            Start with the committed context <strong>“The cat sat”</strong>. A smaller model proposes{' '}
            <strong>“on the mat”</strong>. It is allowed to be wrong. The target model is still responsible for the
            final continuation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <section class="mt-8 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">What happens in one round?</h3>
          <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>
              <strong>Draft.</strong> The small model proposes a short chain and, for exact sampling, supplies its
              proposal distributions.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Verify.</strong> The target evaluates the relevant positions together under causal visibility.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Accept or correct.</strong> For greedy decoding, keep the longest prefix that matches the
              target's choices. At the first mismatch, emit the target's choice and discard the draft tail.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Continue.</strong> Retain or reconstruct state consistent with the committed sequence, then start
              the next round.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Try the middle mismatch first. Then switch to all-match and first-failure. The target predictions in the
            lab are scripted examples, not outputs of an actual LLM.
          </p>

          <figure class="space-y-2">
            <div id="original-lab" class="border border-neutral-400 bg-white">
              <div class="border-b border-neutral-400 px-4 py-3">
                <p class={boxTitle}>Lab 1 / Run a complete greedy round</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xs text-neutral-600">
                  Interactive lesson · illustrative, not a running model
                </p>
              </div>
              <OriginalRoundLab client:load />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-neutral-700">
              <strong>How to read this lab.</strong> The draft is “on the mat.” In the middle-mismatch case, the target
              agrees with “on” but predicts “a” instead of “the.” Commit “on a” and discard “the mat.” Later checks used
              the rejected prefix and cannot be rescued. If all candidates match, a bonus target token can also be
              emitted.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Why the matching prefix matters</h3>
          <p>
            Suppose the target chooses “on,” then “a.” A later prediction after “on the” is no longer a prediction under
            the actual context “on a.” Even if that later token happens to look useful, it cannot be kept on the basis
            of that check.
          </p>
          <p>
            If K candidates all match, the target's logits after the last candidate can provide one additional{' '}
            <strong>bonus token</strong>. The classic round can therefore commit up to K + 1 tokens. Output limits and
            stopping conditions can end the round earlier.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[1]</a> <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[2]</a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">What does the drafter cost?</h3>
          <p>
            A separate draft model needs its own weights, execution, and state. A tiny drafter may be cheap but
            inaccurate; a larger one may be accurate but too expensive. A suitable existing model can be used, or a
            specialized drafter can be trained. The target need not be retrained in the original approach.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compatible token handling is essential. The classic explanation assumes a shared token vocabulary.
            Cross-tokenizer methods require additional machinery and are outside this lesson.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 3 -->
      <section id="verify" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 3</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">How can verification be parallel?</h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            The candidates are available before verification begins. The target can evaluate several sequence positions
            in one multi-position pass, rather than waiting for each next input to be discovered.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full min-w-[20rem] text-left text-sm">
              <thead class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-wide">
                <tr class="border-b border-black">
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold">Candidate being checked</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-bold">Target prediction conditioned on</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-label">on</td>
                  <td class="py-2">The cat sat</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-label">the</td>
                  <td class="py-2">The cat sat on</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-label">mat</td>
                  <td class="py-2">The cat sat on the</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-display font-bold">Bonus token</td>
                  <td class="py-2">The cat sat on the mat</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The causal mask prevents a position from seeing future candidates. Processing “on” produces logits for what
            comes <em>after</em> “on”; it does not prove that “on” itself was correct. The preceding position's logits
            perform that check.
          </p>

          <aside class="border border-l-[7px] border-black bg-white px-4 py-3">
            <p class={boxTitle}>One-position shift</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
              Logits after “sat” check “on.” Logits after “on” check “the.” Logits after “the” check “mat.” Parallel
              scoring does not mean each candidate sees the whole proposed future.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <p>
            If logits after the last committed token are already available, they can check the first candidate.
            Otherwise the engine evaluates the appropriate preceding position as part of the pass. Exact input-buffer
            layout depends on the driver and cache. The conditional predictions above are the invariant.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[1]</a> <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[2]</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">State must follow the accepted answer</h3>
          <p>
            Verification can temporarily compute state for rejected positions. That tail must not become remembered
            context. Standard KV caches can retain the accepted prefix and invalidate the rest. Other architectures may
            require snapshots or replay. A correction token must be processed before later predictions can depend on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Thus selecting, emitting, and processing a token are distinct events. “Parallel verification” refers to
            evaluation across candidate positions, not parallel execution of every layer or independent generation of
            the final answer.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 4 -->
      <section id="sampling" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 4</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">
          Sampling needs a different acceptance rule
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            Greedy verification checks whether a candidate equals the target's highest-scoring token. For stochastic
            sampling, simply comparing independently sampled tokens is not the original exact algorithm.
          </p>
          <p>
            Let <strong>p(x)</strong> be the target probability and <strong>q(x)</strong> the draft probability for the
            same position and prefix. A candidate drawn from q is accepted with probability:
          </p>
          <p class={formula}>min(1, p(x) / q(x))</p>
          <p>If rejected, draw a correction from the normalized positive residual:</p>
          <p class={formula}>r(x) = max(0, p(x) − q(x)) / Σ<sub>y</sub> max(0, p(y) − q(y))</p>
          <p>
            The correction compensates for probability mass that the draft overproduced. It is not an arbitrary
            fallback. The original papers prove distribution preservation under the algorithm's assumptions. Define p
            and q after the intended sampling transformations.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[1]</a> <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[2]</a>
          </p>

          <figure class="space-y-2">
            <div id="sampling-lab" class="border border-neutral-400 bg-white">
              <div class="border-b border-neutral-400 px-4 py-3">
                <p class={boxTitle}>Lab 2 / Keep the target distribution</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xs text-neutral-600">
                  Interactive lesson · illustrative, not a running model
                </p>
              </div>
              <SamplingLab client:load />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-neutral-700">
              <strong>How to read this lab.</strong> With target A=60% and draft A=80%, accept a draft A with
              probability 75%. Draft B is always accepted. Rejected A is corrected to B. The final output distribution
              is A=60%, B=40%. These bars are exact calculations for a hypothetical two-token vocabulary, not a random
              simulation.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <details class="border border-neutral-400 bg-neutral-100 px-4 py-3">
            <summary class={questionCls}>What if p and q are equal?</summary>
            <p class="mt-2">
              Every proposal is accepted. The residual has zero mass, but no rejection occurs, so it does not need to
              be sampled. The lab handles this case explicitly.
            </p>
          </details>

          <aside class="border border-dashed border-neutral-600 px-4 py-3">
            <p class={boxTitle}>Exact distribution is not identical text for the same seed</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
              Random numbers may be consumed differently, so distribution equality does not imply seed-by-seed output
              equality. Greedy equality also assumes the same target arithmetic, settings, and tie-breaking. A relaxed
              acceptance rule needs a separate quality claim.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 5 -->
      <section id="medusa" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 5</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">
          Medusa: predict future positions with extra heads
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            The original method must find and serve a suitable smaller model. Medusa changes the proposal mechanism:
            attach additional decoding heads to the target's final hidden representations.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>hidden representation</strong> is a numerical vector produced inside the model. A{' '}
            <strong>head</strong> maps that representation into predictions. The ordinary LM head predicts the next
            token. Medusa's extra heads predict positions farther ahead from the same representation; one head does not
            simply consume the previous head's chosen token.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[3, section 2.1]</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <section class="mt-8 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">One round</h3>
          <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>Obtain the target's hidden representation at the current boundary.</li>
            <li>The LM head and extra heads produce candidate distributions for their designated future positions.</li>
            <li>Take selected top candidates and arrange possible continuations as a tree.</li>
            <li>The target evaluates tree positions together using a branch-aware attention mask and matching position indices.</li>
            <li>Apply the chosen acceptance policy, commit a continuation, and use accepted target representations for the next round.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A tree shares prefixes across alternatives. A node may attend to its ancestors and the committed context,
            but not to another branch. More branches offer more chances of a useful continuation and also create more
            verification work.
          </p>

          <figure class="space-y-2">
            <div id="medusa-lab" class="border border-neutral-400 bg-white">
              <div class="border-b border-neutral-400 px-4 py-3">
                <p class={boxTitle}>Lab 3 / Inspect a candidate tree</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xs text-neutral-600">
                  Interactive lesson · illustrative, not a running model
                </p>
              </div>
              <MedusaTreeLab client:load />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-neutral-700">
              <strong>How to read this lab.</strong> The LM head supplies “sat.” Additional heads propose
              future-position candidates from the same hidden representation. Nodes see the committed prefix and their
              ancestors, not sibling branches. In this scripted greedy case the target path is “sat on a”; “near” and
              “the” branches are rejected. The widget teaches tree visibility and greedy selection, not Medusa's
              typical-acceptance policy.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Two training strategies</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Medusa-1</strong> freezes the backbone and trains the additional heads. <strong>Medusa-2</strong>{' '}
            trains heads and backbone together, with a training recipe intended to preserve the backbone's next-token
            capability. Training the backbone changes which target is being accelerated; do not compare it as if the
            original weights were untouched. <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[3, section 2.2]</a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Acceptance is a separate choice</h3>
          <p>
            The paper discusses rejection sampling and <strong>typical acceptance</strong>. Typical acceptance keeps
            candidates whose target probability exceeds a threshold related to probability and entropy, rather than
            requiring the exact original sampling distribution. It seeks similar quality with more acceptance; it is not
            the same guarantee as exact speculative sampling. The widget deliberately uses a simple greedy rule so the
            tree mechanics stay clear. <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[3, section 2.3]</a>
          </p>

          <aside class="border border-l-[7px] border-black bg-white px-4 py-3">
            <p class={boxTitle}>What changed? What stayed?</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
              Changed: proposal generation uses trained heads and a candidate tree. Stayed: the target evaluates
              continuations, and rejected branches cannot become committed state. The acceptance policy determines the
              guarantee.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <p>
            Use this approach when trained heads and tree-verification support are available. Its advantages are shared
            target representations and no standalone small-model backbone. Its costs include head training, additional
            parameters, and potentially large candidate trees.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 6 -->
      <section id="eagle" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 6</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">
          EAGLE: predict features with the selected token in view
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            EAGLE changes what the drafter predicts. Instead of reproducing a complete small language model, it learns
            to predict target <strong>features</strong>: hidden representations before the LM head. The original EAGLE
            paper combines a feature sequence with a token sequence shifted one step ahead. This section covers that
            original design, not every later EAGLE version. <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[4]</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <section class="mt-8 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Why feature-only prediction is ambiguous</h3>
          <p>
            After “I,” the target may select “am” or “always.” Those choices create different next representations. The
            feature after “I” alone does not specify which branch was selected. EAGLE gives the draft component the
            selected next token, so it predicts the feature for that particular continuation.
          </p>
          <p>
            For example: the target feature after “I” plus the selected token “am” helps predict a feature after “I am.”
            Passing that predicted feature through the target LM head proposes the following token, such as “ready.” The
            drafter can then use its predicted feature and proposed token to continue.
          </p>

          <figure class="space-y-2">
            <div id="eagle-lab" class="border border-neutral-400 bg-white">
              <div class="border-b border-neutral-400 px-4 py-3">
                <p class={boxTitle}>Lab 4 / Follow a feature-based draft</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xs text-neutral-600">
                  Interactive lesson · illustrative, not a running model
                </p>
              </div>
              <EagleFeatureLab client:load />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-neutral-700">
              <strong>How to read this lab.</strong> The feature after “I” alone does not identify the next feature:
              choosing “am” and choosing “always” lead to different contexts. EAGLE conditions feature prediction on the
              selected next token as well. Its predicted feature is passed through the target LM head to propose a later
              token. The symbolic features are explanatory labels, not real model vectors. This widget follows one
              branch; the original method can draft a tree.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">One round in the original method</h3>
          <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>Collect target features for the current accepted sequence.</li>
            <li>Embed the one-step-ahead tokens and combine them with the feature sequence.</li>
            <li>A trained autoregression component predicts the next feature.</li>
            <li>The target's LM head maps that predicted feature into a token distribution.</li>
            <li>Repeat cheap drafting, potentially expanding a candidate tree.</li>
            <li>The target verifies the tree and applies the speculative sampling rule. Accepted tokens and actual target features seed the next round.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The original draft component uses a projection and a decoder layer. The target embedding and LM head are
            reused rather than newly trained; the target backbone remains fixed. Training uses feature regression and a
            token-distribution objective, with noise augmentation to reduce sensitivity to accumulated feature errors.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[4, section 3]</a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Guarantee and trade-off</h3>
          <p>
            Feature predictions may be inaccurate. Exactness comes from target verification and the
            acceptance/correction algorithm, not from perfect feature prediction. The original method uses tree
            verification with speculative sampling to preserve the target distribution.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[4, section 3.3]</a>
          </p>

          <aside class="border border-l-[7px] border-black bg-white px-4 py-3">
            <p class={boxTitle}>What changed? What stayed?</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
              Changed: the drafter predicts target-like features conditioned on shifted token information. Stayed:
              candidates are provisional, the target verifies them, and the exact acceptance rule protects the target
              distribution.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <p>
            This requires a trained component matched to the target and an engine able to expose and reuse the relevant
            representations. It is not a generic plugin for any checkpoint without preparation. Feature error can
            accumulate across draft steps, so cheapness and acceptance still need measurement.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 7 -->
      <section id="layerskip" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 7</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">
          LayerSkip: use early layers as the drafter
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            LayerSkip uses one model at two depths. The early layers propose tokens, and the remaining layers verify
            those proposals. It avoids a separate draft model and can reuse early-layer computation.
          </p>
          <p>
            An <strong>early exit</strong> reads a prediction before the final layer. Ordinary models are not
            necessarily trained to make useful predictions at every early layer. LayerSkip addresses this with layer
            dropout and an early-exit loss using a shared LM head. Later layers are dropped more often during training,
            encouraging earlier layers to become useful predictors.{' '}
            <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[5, section 4.1]</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <section class="mt-8 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">One round</h3>
          <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>Choose an exit layer in a suitably trained model.</li>
            <li>Run the early layers autoregressively to propose several tokens.</li>
            <li>Retain their boundary activations and early-layer cache.</li>
            <li>Run the remaining layers on those positions together to obtain full-depth target predictions.</li>
            <li>Accept or correct, then repair the cache to match the committed continuation.</li>
          </ol>

          <figure class="space-y-2">
            <div id="layer-lab" class="border border-neutral-400 bg-white">
              <div class="border-b border-neutral-400 px-4 py-3">
                <p class={boxTitle}>Lab 5 / Choose an early exit</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xs text-neutral-600">
                  Interactive lesson · illustrative, not a running model
                </p>
              </div>
              <LayerSkipLab client:load />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-neutral-700">
              <strong>How to read this lab.</strong> This conceptual model has 12 layers. Exiting after layer 4 uses
              layers 1–4 for cheap drafting. Verification continues stored boundary activations through layers 5–12 for
              multiple candidate positions. Moving the split changes the division of work; it does not supply a measured
              speedup or acceptance rate. A LayerSkip-trained checkpoint is required for the intended early-exit
              behavior.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Why computation can be shared</h3>
          <p>
            The verification path uses the same early layers in the same order as the draft path. Their results need not
            be discarded and recomputed from scratch. The paper's self-verification stage continues from retained
            activations through the later layers. Its cache handling keeps the accepted prefix and rolls back rejected
            work. <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[5, sections 4.3.1–4.3.3]</a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Early exit alone is not self-speculative decoding</h3>
          <p>
            If you simply stop at an early layer and output its token, you can change the model's behavior.
            Self-speculation adds full-depth verification and correction. The relevant target is the full-depth version
            of the trained checkpoint, not an unrelated pre-training checkpoint.
          </p>

          <aside class="border border-l-[7px] border-black bg-white px-4 py-3">
            <p class={boxTitle}>What changed? What stayed?</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
              Changed: the draft and target share one backbone, split by depth, with reusable activations. Stayed: the
              full-depth target verifies the proposed continuation, and rejected state must be repaired.
            </p>
          </aside>

          <p>
            An earlier exit can make drafting cheaper but less accurate; a later exit can improve proposals but spend
            more work before verification. The optimum depends on the trained model, workload, hardware, and draft
            length. The widget shows the split only, not a fabricated accuracy curve.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 8 -->
      <section id="performance" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 8</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">
          Choose a method by cost, not by its name
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            Speculation may do more arithmetic yet finish sooner by reducing expensive serial target calls. This is
            especially plausible when short-batch target decode is constrained by data movement and a short
            multi-position verification pass can reuse weights effectively. It is not a universal property of every
            workload. <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[1]</a> <a href="#sources" class={cite}>[2]</a>
          </p>
          <p>
            Acceptance rate alone is not enough. You need draft cost, verification cost, state handling, and useful
            committed tokens. Tree methods also propose alternatives that cannot all be accepted, so their raw
            accepted/drafted ratio is not directly comparable with a chain ratio.
          </p>

          <figure class="space-y-2">
            <div id="performance-lab" class="border border-neutral-400 bg-white">
              <div class="border-b border-neutral-400 px-4 py-3">
                <p class={boxTitle}>Lab 6 / Does the extra work pay off?</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xs text-neutral-600">
                  Interactive lesson · illustrative, not a running model
                </p>
              </div>
              <PerformanceLab client:load />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-neutral-700">
              <strong>How to read this lab.</strong> All timings are hypothetical inputs. A round costing 18 ms that
              commits four tokens averages 4.5 ms per token, compared with 10 ms ordinary decode. If it commits only
              one, it loses. This simplified calculation excludes prefill, queueing, and variable round costs; it is not
              a hardware forecast.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full min-w-[32rem] text-left text-sm">
              <thead class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-wide">
                <tr class="border-b border-black">
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-3 font-bold">Method</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-3 font-bold">Proposal source</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-3 font-bold">Preparation</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-bold">Main practical cost</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-display font-bold">Original</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Separate small model</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Compatible existing or trained drafter</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Extra model weights, state, and draft execution</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-display font-bold">Medusa</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Future-position heads</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Head training; optionally joint backbone training</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Heads and candidate-tree verification</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-neutral-400">
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-display font-bold">Original EAGLE</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Feature predictor plus shifted tokens</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Target-specific draft training</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Feature integration and tree/state management</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-display font-bold">LayerSkip</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Early layers of the same model</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-3">Early-exit training recipe</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Split-depth execution and cache repair</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Longer drafts can increase useful accepted tokens and wasted tail work. More concurrency can reduce the
            spare compute that made speculation attractive. Streaming may become burstier because several tokens are
            committed after one check. Decode speedup does not automatically improve end-to-end first-token time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">A fair inference-engine experiment</h3>
          <p>
            Hold target checkpoint, backend, precision, prompts, output limits, sampling, cache conditions, and
            concurrency comparable. Record actual output length, client latency, failures, memory, draft/verify costs,
            and committed tokens per round. For a changed backbone, say explicitly that the target changed. Check output
            consistency under the promised acceptance policy.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not assume all four methods are supported by a given engine. A checkpoint needs the appropriate trained
            components and the engine needs the matching execution path. MTP is a training or prediction capability, not
            a synonym for one universal speculative algorithm.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <!-- Chapter 9 -->
      <section id="recap" class="mt-12 border-t-[3px] border-black pt-4">
        <p class={label}>Chapter 9</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight">Teach back the whole mechanism</h2>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-800">
          <p>
            A good drafter is cheap and often useful, not authoritative. Verification evaluates conditional predictions
            under candidate prefixes. Acceptance selects a valid continuation, and state repair ensures the next round
            starts from that continuation.
          </p>

          <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>Why can candidate positions be checked together?</li>
            <li>Why can a matching token after the first rejection not be kept?</li>
            <li>What changes between Medusa heads and original EAGLE's draft component?</li>
            <li>Why does LayerSkip need special training?</li>
            <li>What does exact sampling guarantee, and what does it not guarantee?</li>
          </ol>

          <details class="border border-neutral-400 bg-neutral-100 px-4 py-3">
            <summary class={questionCls}>Read the answer key</summary>
            <ol class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
              <li>
                The candidate inputs are already known. Causal evaluation can score their conditional predictions
                together without choosing unknown inputs first.
              </li>
              <li>Its check used a rejected prefix. The corrected prefix may give a different prediction.</li>
              <li>
                Medusa heads predict future-position tokens from shared target representations. Original EAGLE predicts
                features conditioned on shifted token information, then uses the LM head to propose tokens.
              </li>
              <li>
                Early layers and the shared head need to make useful early predictions. Ordinary final-layer training
                does not ensure that.
              </li>
              <li>
                It preserves the target distribution under the algorithm's assumptions. It does not imply identical text
                for the same seed, universal speedup, or exactness for relaxed acceptance policies.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </details>

          <aside class="border border-l-[7px] border-black bg-white px-4 py-3">
            <p class={boxTitle}>The lasting takeaway</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
              Speculative decoding prepares a possible future so the target can check it efficiently. Parallel
              computation and sequential acceptance cooperate; neither turns the guesses into independent final answers.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p class="mt-12 border-t border-black pt-4 text-sm italic text-neutral-700">
        Read the original algorithm first, then the method whose proposal mechanism interests you. This guide describes
        the cited versions, not a complete or current survey of every speculative-decoding variant. All token examples,
        target choices, feature symbols, layer counts in widgets, and timing inputs are illustrative. The widgets do not
        execute an LLM, reproduce training, or report benchmark results. Their static captions preserve the lesson
        without JavaScript.
      </p>

      <div id="sources" class="mt-8">
        <Sources items={sources} heading="Sources and reading paths" />
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
